td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/2129"} "Sea Forest Waterway, 6-44, 3chome, Uminomori, Koto-ku, Tokyo Bay, Tokyo, Japan"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/19016245"} "40 from 16 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "This was the 16th appearance of this event, having appeared at every Summer Games since 1960. The reigning world champions were " [:a {:href "/athletes/121063"} "Maryna Litvinchuk"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/121064"} "Volha Khudzenka"] " of Belarus. Other medal contenders were the 2017 world champions " [:a {:href "/athletes/126097"} "Lisa Carrington"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/131267"} "Caitlin Regal"] " of New Zealand, and reigning Olympic champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/116671"} "Danuta Kozák"] " of Hungary, now paired with " [:a {:href "/athletes/143620"} "Dóra Bodonyi"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "All the favourites easily qualified directly into the semi-finals. Carrington and Regal won the first semi while Kozák and Bodonyi won the second semi to set up an interesting battle for the gold medal. The final was the fourth race (and second final) of the day for Carrington."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Just 90 minutes after winning gold in the K-1 200, Carrington jumped back in the boat and won the K-2 500 race. Poland’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/122362"} "Karolina Naja"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/145924"} "Anna Pulawska"] " followed in the silver medal position, with Kozák and Bodonyi pipping compatriots " [:a {:href "/athletes/130506"} "Tamara Csipes"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/143623"} "Erika Medveczky"] " to the bronze medal. Naja had already won bronze with a different partner in this discipline in London and Rio. Winning two gold medals in one day took Carrington’s career total to four Olympic golds, just one behind her main rival, Kozák."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "NOC"] [